<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">

    <!--
        Declared here rather than in the app module on purpose: the module that needs the
        permission declares it, so removing :speech removes the permission with it. Library
        manifests are merged into the app's at build time.

        RECORD_AUDIO is a runtime permission. The request flow, its Arabic rationale, and the
        permanently-denied path are UI concerns and live in feature-chat — see
        docs/M2_DESIGN_NOTE.md §6. This line only makes the permission requestable.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.RECORD_AUDIO" />

    <!--
        Not for networking — Ktor needs no permission to open a socket. This exists solely so a
        failed Deepgram handshake can be told apart from having no connection at all.

        On 2026-08-06 a deliberately wrong key produced «تحقّق من اتصالك» on a device with full
        signal, because every handshake failure was being reported as NetworkRequired. That is
        the fault this whole day was spent on, in miniature: the app reported the symptom it saw
        instead of the cause. Asking the system whether a network exists turns one ambiguous
        failure into two honest ones.

        Normal permission — granted at install, no runtime prompt.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.ACCESS_NETWORK_STATE" />

    <!--
        Android 11+ package visibility: without this, SpeechRecognizer.isRecognitionAvailable()
        can report false on a device that does have an engine, because the app cannot see the
        package that provides it. Silent, confusing, and it would present as "no engine".
    -->
    <queries>
        <intent>
            <action android:name="android.speech.RecognitionService" />
        </intent>
    </queries>

</manifest>
